Method:

public List<RRset> getSectionRRsets(int section) {
    Section.check(section);
    if (sections[section] == null) {
        return Collections.emptyList();
    }
    List<RRset> sets = new LinkedList<>();
    record_iteration : for (Record rec : sections[section]) {
        for (int j = sets.size() - 1; j >= 0; j--) {
            RRset set = sets.get(j);
            if (rec.sameRRset(set)) {
                set.addRR(rec);
                // Existing set found, continue with the next record
                continue record_iteration;
            }
        }
        // No existing set found, create a new one
        sets.add(new RRset(rec));
    }
    return sets;
}

Potential exceptions:

- NullPointerException if sections array itself is null. In class, sections is declared private List<Record>[] sections; Not initialized in snippet. In constructor maybe set. If sections is null, sections[section] would NPE. Not covered.

- ArrayIndexOutOfBoundsException if sections length is less than section index (but Section.check ensures valid values 0-3). However if sections array length is less than 4 (maybe not initialized), accessing sections[section] could cause AIOOBE. That could be considered an exception scenario.

- NullPointerException if a Record in sections[section] is null; the for-each loop would iterate over list containing null, then rec.sameRRset(set) would NPE. So test case for null record in list.

- NullPointerException if RRset constructor receives null record (new RRset(rec)). If rec is null, same as above.

- Potential IllegalStateException from Section.check? Already covered.

- If sections[section] is non-null but contains a Record whose sameRRset throws an exception (unlikely). But we can test behavior when Record.sameRRset throws RuntimeException, but we cannot guarantee. However we can simulate using a mock Record that throws.

